1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,640
A man walked out of the most famous
museum in the world with its most

2
00:00:03,660 --> 00:00:06,090
famous painting under his arm.

3
00:00:06,360 --> 00:00:10,100
A painter nobody took seriously
sold a fake masterpiece to a Nazi

4
00:00:10,120 --> 00:00:13,610
leader for 1.65 million guilders.

5
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:16,460
A magazine paid about nine
million marks for secret

6
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:19,270
diaries that had the wrong
initials on the cover.

7
00:00:19,420 --> 00:00:23,700
And a wine collector set a world
auction record years before prosecutors

8
00:00:23,720 --> 00:00:26,350
called his own house a wine factory.

9
00:00:26,540 --> 00:00:30,360
Four stories, three fakes that
fooled the experts and one real

10
00:00:30,380 --> 00:00:33,270
masterpiece that experts
had to prove was real.

11
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:37,060
Every one of them ended in a courtroom
and every one of them came apart

12
00:00:37,080 --> 00:00:38,830
over something tiny.

13
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:42,660
Stay to the end because there are
three simple checks anyone can do and

14
00:00:42,680 --> 00:00:44,630
they would have caught all four.

15
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:47,770
Chapter one, the Mona Lisa thief.

16
00:00:48,440 --> 00:00:51,610
Paris, Monday, August 21st, 1911.

17
00:00:51,940 --> 00:00:54,310
The Louvre Museum is closed for the day.

18
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:56,760
A man named Vincenzo Peruggia walks in.

19
00:00:56,780 --> 00:00:59,820
He's Italian and he used
to work at the Louvre.

20
00:00:59,840 --> 00:01:04,070
He takes the Mona Lisa off the wall
and walks out with it under his arm.

21
00:01:04,140 --> 00:01:05,990
How long until anyone noticed?

22
00:01:06,120 --> 00:01:07,570
A whole day.

23
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:10,380
The next day, a painter
notices four empty hooks

24
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:11,900
where the painting should be.

25
00:01:11,920 --> 00:01:13,990
The Louvre shuts for a week.

26
00:01:14,280 --> 00:01:18,040
Newspapers around the world print the
empty wall and crowds line up just

27
00:01:18,060 --> 00:01:19,920
to stare at the blank spot.

28
00:01:19,940 --> 00:01:21,490
And then, nothing.

29
00:01:21,620 --> 00:01:23,340
Not for weeks, not for months.

30
00:01:23,360 --> 00:01:27,340
For over two years, nobody
finds it because the most

31
00:01:27,360 --> 00:01:31,770
famous painting on Earth is sitting
in Peruggia's small Paris apartment.

32
00:01:32,080 --> 00:01:34,650
So how do you catch a
thief who left no trail?

33
00:01:34,720 --> 00:01:35,540
You don't.

34
00:01:35,560 --> 00:01:36,890
He catches himself.

35
00:01:37,140 --> 00:01:40,560
In 1913, he writes a letter
to an art dealer in Florence,

36
00:01:40,580 --> 00:01:42,510
Italy, named Alfredo Geri.

37
00:01:42,820 --> 00:01:46,240
He offers him the Mona
Lisa for 500,000 lire and

38
00:01:46,260 --> 00:01:48,470
says the painting should go home to Italy.

39
00:01:48,760 --> 00:01:51,400
Geri brings in an expert,
the director of the Uffizi

40
00:01:51,420 --> 00:01:53,440
Gallery, Giovanni Poggi.

41
00:01:53,460 --> 00:01:55,330
They meet Peruggia at his hotel.

42
00:01:55,400 --> 00:01:57,250
They look at the painting closely.

43
00:01:57,400 --> 00:01:58,840
And it's the real one.

44
00:01:58,860 --> 00:02:00,830
They tell him they'll
take it to be checked.

45
00:02:00,920 --> 00:02:05,630
Then, in mid-December 1913, the
police arrest him at his hotel.

46
00:02:05,740 --> 00:02:10,730
In 1914, a court in Florence
sentenced him to one year and 15 days.

47
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:14,490
He said he did it for Italy and he
ended up serving less than a year.

48
00:02:14,660 --> 00:02:16,280
And here's the strange part.

49
00:02:16,300 --> 00:02:18,910
Before the theft, the
Mona Lisa was famous.

50
00:02:18,990 --> 00:02:22,530
After it, it was the most
famous painting in the world.

51
00:02:22,740 --> 00:02:26,570
Hit like if you can't believe the
thief got caught by his own letter.

52
00:02:27,060 --> 00:02:29,570
Chapter two, the Vermeer Forger.

53
00:02:29,780 --> 00:02:32,260
The Netherlands, the 1930s.

54
00:02:32,280 --> 00:02:35,590
Han van Meegeren is a Dutch
painter the critics ignore.

55
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:41,280
So, he picks a target, Johannes
Vermeer, a Dutch master from the 1600s.

56
00:02:41,300 --> 00:02:45,260
Only a few dozen real Vermeers are
known, so a new one would be like

57
00:02:45,280 --> 00:02:46,530
buried treasure.

58
00:02:46,580 --> 00:02:50,680
Van Meegeren paints new pictures in
Vermeer's style, then sells them as

59
00:02:50,700 --> 00:02:53,130
lost Vermeers that were just discovered.

60
00:02:53,300 --> 00:02:55,290
And the art world believes him.

61
00:02:55,580 --> 00:02:57,390
Experts and museums buy them.

62
00:02:57,580 --> 00:02:59,330
Then, it gets dangerous.

63
00:02:59,720 --> 00:03:02,490
In 1943, during the German occupation,

64
00:03:02,540 --> 00:03:06,060
one of his fakes is sold through a
dealer to Hermann Goering, a Nazi

65
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:06,850
leader.

66
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:07,800
The price?

67
00:03:07,820 --> 00:03:10,630
1.65 million guilders.

68
00:03:10,700 --> 00:03:14,110
Guilders were Dutch money back
then, and this was a fortune.

69
00:03:14,200 --> 00:03:18,700
In 1945, after the war, investigators
find the painting in Goering's

70
00:03:18,720 --> 00:03:21,290
collection and trace it
back to Van Meegeren.

71
00:03:21,420 --> 00:03:25,460
To them, it looks like he sold a
Dutch national treasure to the enemy.

72
00:03:25,480 --> 00:03:28,100
So, he's arrested and
charged with collaboration,

73
00:03:28,120 --> 00:03:29,810
which means helping the enemy.

74
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:31,560
So, how do you get out of that?

75
00:03:31,580 --> 00:03:33,410
You confess to something smaller.

76
00:03:33,580 --> 00:03:36,080
Van Meegeren says, I
didn't sell a treasure,

77
00:03:36,100 --> 00:03:37,550
I painted it myself.

78
00:03:37,900 --> 00:03:39,120
Nobody believes him.

79
00:03:39,140 --> 00:03:41,810
The experts had already
called those paintings real.

80
00:03:41,920 --> 00:03:44,490
So, he asks for paint and a canvas.

81
00:03:44,620 --> 00:03:48,160
And while officials watch, he paints
a brand new picture in Vermeer's

82
00:03:48,180 --> 00:03:48,970
style.

83
00:03:49,300 --> 00:03:50,740
Now, the charge changes.

84
00:03:50,760 --> 00:03:53,050
He's not a traitor, he's a forger.

85
00:03:53,200 --> 00:03:57,340
In 1947, he's convicted of
forgery and fraud and sentenced to

86
00:03:57,360 --> 00:03:58,890
one year in prison.

87
00:03:59,160 --> 00:04:02,400
Weeks later, he dies
before he ever serves it.

88
00:04:02,420 --> 00:04:04,620
The experts couldn't spot his fakes.

89
00:04:04,640 --> 00:04:07,820
In the end, the only way to prove
they were fake was to watch him

90
00:04:07,840 --> 00:04:08,760
paint one.

91
00:04:08,780 --> 00:04:12,380
And if you've got a friend who
swears they'd never fall for a fake,

92
00:04:12,400 --> 00:04:13,960
send them this one.

93
00:04:13,980 --> 00:04:16,790
Chapter three, the fake diaries.

94
00:04:17,100 --> 00:04:19,760
West Germany, the early 1980s.

95
00:04:19,780 --> 00:04:23,990
A man named Konrad Kujau sells
old war souvenirs in Stuttgart.

96
00:04:24,120 --> 00:04:26,780
Then, he starts writing notebooks by hand,

97
00:04:26,800 --> 00:04:27,780
dozens of them.

98
00:04:27,800 --> 00:04:30,670
And he claims they're
Hitler's secret diaries.

99
00:04:30,840 --> 00:04:33,730
He sells them under a fake
name through a middleman.

100
00:04:33,860 --> 00:04:36,520
They end up with Gerd
Heidemann, a star reporter

101
00:04:36,540 --> 00:04:39,240
at Stern, one of Germany's
biggest magazines.

102
00:04:39,260 --> 00:04:41,230
And he believes they're real.

103
00:04:41,440 --> 00:04:45,050
He talks his bosses into paying
about nine million marks.

104
00:04:45,320 --> 00:04:49,010
Marks were West Germany's money
back then, so this was a fortune.

105
00:04:49,220 --> 00:04:53,390
And Heidemann, he secretly kept a
big chunk of that money for himself.

106
00:04:53,660 --> 00:04:57,570
In April 1983, the magazine
tells the whole world.

107
00:04:57,660 --> 00:05:00,600
But nobody had really checked
the most obvious thing,

108
00:05:00,620 --> 00:05:01,530
the cover.

109
00:05:01,740 --> 00:05:05,100
On the front of the notebooks were
plastic letters bought from a shop,

110
00:05:05,120 --> 00:05:07,160
the letters F and H.

111
00:05:07,180 --> 00:05:09,120
But the name starts with an A.

112
00:05:09,140 --> 00:05:10,990
It should have said A-H.

113
00:05:11,360 --> 00:05:15,140
Then, Germany's National Archives
tested the paper and the ink.

114
00:05:15,160 --> 00:05:17,930
Both were modern, far too new to be real.

115
00:05:17,980 --> 00:05:20,980
And it took them less than two
weeks after the big announcement.

116
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:23,660
They called the diaries a
crude forgery, which means

117
00:05:23,680 --> 00:05:25,690
a cheap, obvious fake.

118
00:05:25,840 --> 00:05:30,120
In 1985, a court in Hamburg
convicted Kujau and Heidemann.

119
00:05:30,140 --> 00:05:33,170
Each was sentenced to about
four and a half years in prison.

120
00:05:33,320 --> 00:05:36,920
So the money was paid first
and the cheap test came after.

121
00:05:36,940 --> 00:05:40,470
Flip that order and the fake
probably never makes it to print.

122
00:05:41,120 --> 00:05:43,890
Chapter four, the wine in the kitchen.

123
00:05:44,140 --> 00:05:46,540
Los Angeles, the early 2000s.

124
00:05:46,560 --> 00:05:50,460
A young collector named Rudy Kurniawan
becomes a star of the rare wine

125
00:05:50,480 --> 00:05:51,230
world.

126
00:05:51,360 --> 00:05:54,850
He opens rare old bottles at fancy
dinners like they're nothing.

127
00:05:55,040 --> 00:05:59,690
In 2006, two auctions of his
wine bring in about $35 million.

128
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:02,310
The second one sets a world record.

129
00:06:02,460 --> 00:06:06,410
So how do you fake a wine that the
world's best tasters are drinking?

130
00:06:06,980 --> 00:06:08,680
Prosecutors say it worked like this.

131
00:06:08,700 --> 00:06:12,660
He took older, cheaper wine, mixed
it with newer wine and poured it into

132
00:06:12,680 --> 00:06:13,810
old bottles.

133
00:06:14,020 --> 00:06:18,290
Then came new corks, new wax and
printed labels for famous wines.

134
00:06:18,460 --> 00:06:22,210
And for years, plenty of experts
drank it and bought more.

135
00:06:22,340 --> 00:06:23,700
So what caught him?

136
00:06:23,720 --> 00:06:25,770
Not taste, a date.

137
00:06:25,940 --> 00:06:29,820
In 2008, a New York auction listed
his bottles of a French wine called

138
00:06:29,840 --> 00:06:33,510
Clos Saint-Denis with
labels going back to 1945.

139
00:06:33,900 --> 00:06:37,680
The winemaker, Laurent
Ponsot, saw that and objected.

140
00:06:37,700 --> 00:06:40,870
His family didn't start
making that wine until 1982.

141
00:06:41,320 --> 00:06:45,010
So those labels were 37 years
older than the wine itself.

142
00:06:45,140 --> 00:06:47,110
The bottles were pulled from the sale.

143
00:06:47,340 --> 00:06:51,690
In 2012, the FBI arrested Kurniawan
at his home in California.

144
00:06:52,100 --> 00:06:56,670
Inside were old bottles, corks, wax
and thousands of printed labels.

145
00:06:57,060 --> 00:06:59,090
Prosecutors called it a wine factory.

146
00:06:59,500 --> 00:07:01,740
Billionaire collector
Bill Koch told the jury he

147
00:07:01,760 --> 00:07:07,190
paid $2.1 million for 219
bottles he says were fake.

148
00:07:07,560 --> 00:07:11,660
In 2013, a Manhattan jury
convicted Kurniawan of fraud.

149
00:07:11,680 --> 00:07:15,020
He was sentenced to 10 years in
prison and ordered to pay back about

150
00:07:15,040 --> 00:07:17,090
$28.4 million.

151
00:07:17,560 --> 00:07:20,200
The best noses in the
business didn't catch it.

152
00:07:20,220 --> 00:07:22,050
A guy reading a label did.

153
00:07:22,360 --> 00:07:25,070
So what do all four have in common?

154
00:07:25,340 --> 00:07:27,770
Look at what actually gave each one away.

155
00:07:27,820 --> 00:07:30,300
A thief who wrote a
letter asking for money.

156
00:07:30,320 --> 00:07:34,510
A forger who had to paint one more
fake to prove the others were fake.

157
00:07:34,700 --> 00:07:36,550
Two plastic letters on a cover.

158
00:07:36,840 --> 00:07:40,550
And a date on a label that
was 37 years too early.

159
00:07:40,780 --> 00:07:42,750
None of them took a genius detective.

160
00:07:42,820 --> 00:07:46,050
Everyone came down to a
detail anyone could check.

161
00:07:46,140 --> 00:07:49,360
So here are three simple
checks anyone can do.

162
00:07:49,380 --> 00:07:51,930
One, read the boring details.

163
00:07:51,980 --> 00:07:54,710
The date, the spelling,
the name on the front.

164
00:07:54,840 --> 00:07:57,020
Two, ask where it came from.

165
00:07:57,040 --> 00:08:01,950
A secret seller or a lost treasure
that just showed up means slow down.

166
00:08:02,200 --> 00:08:05,360
Three, check it before you pay, not after.

167
00:08:05,380 --> 00:08:07,980
The magazine paid first and tested later.

168
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:09,910
That order cost them millions.

169
00:08:10,220 --> 00:08:13,350
Those three checks would have
caught every fake in this video.

170
00:08:13,520 --> 00:08:17,640
If you want more stories where
one tiny detail sinks a giant lie,

171
00:08:17,660 --> 00:08:18,430
subscribe.

172
00:08:18,480 --> 00:08:20,170
There's a new one every week.

